# Bound background work to preserve API responsiveness

byre task tskGQp200m5Jyi, "Server workload isolation", created by ian@fc.run, merged by ian@fc.run.
Full chats: the events of task tskGQp200m5Jyi in byre (GET /api/events?task_id=tskGQp200m5Jyi).

## Summary

The task began with an authorized production import and document catch-up. Duplicate PDF rendering and production overload during that work led to the server workload-isolation change. Production catch-up was paused; it remains stopped.

Before: identical PDFs could be rendered and uploaded repeatedly.
Now: the uploader reuses page image and server-thumbnail CONTENT references through ordinary write batches, using the PDF content ID as identity. It uploads pages, lets the server thumbnail them, then copies both references. No custom reuse endpoint remains. This earlier work was merged during the task.

Before: image processing, search rebuilding, refreshes and background commits competed independently with client API requests.
Now: expensive phases share one in-process CPU budget with foreground priority, serial search training and idle intervals based on actual process CPU use. Background commits acquire CPU and commit admission atomically; network I/O holds no CPU permit. Interactive HTTP writes retain priority even under a system audit identity. Membership warming does not wait on an occupied cache while holding CPU admission.

Before: refresh admission waits blocked persistence, and refresh requests arriving during an active pass could be discarded.
Now: preparation and swapping run off the persistence worker, flushed plans remain available for replay, and requests during a pass schedule a reread. Flush and drain account for those passes. Cancellation during PNG validation is preserved rather than reported as invalid image input.

In a local production-snapshot test using two CPU threads, foreground edit maximum latency fell from 1.19s to 595ms and warmed-query maximum from 138ms to 1.3ms; background completions fell from 30 to 11 over the same interval. Snapshot data was deleted. Store/scheduler race tests, targeted database race checks, database tests, app/access/mail tests and repository-wide vet passed. Main merged without conflicts or substantive adjustments; the final database verification ran in two batches after the combined run exceeded the 30-second command limit.

Deliberately excluded: separate worker processes, hard memory isolation, preemption of running transactions, removal of the client's backlog checks, production deployment/restart and renewed production catch-up. A separate roughly 800ms cold membership-cache query cost remains.

